window.docContents[43].resolve({"/#leanpde":{"contents":"PDEAtlas is a formalization and exposition of Lawrence C. Evans's\nPartial Differential Equations, second edition,\nin Lean 4 and Mathlib. It pairs selected results from the textbook with\nmachine-checked proofs, worked calculations, and explanations of the analysis.\nThe exposition is intended for graduate students studying PDEs and for\nundergraduates with a background in analysis.\n\nPDEAtlas is also a foundation for further PDE formalization. Its Sobolev spaces,\nweak derivatives, energy estimates, and solution constructions are designed\nfor reuse in projects on Navier–Stokes, Euler, and singularity formation.\nOur proof-producing CAS connects symbolic calculus and certified numerical\nbounds to those analytic arguments, including the De Gregorio blowup proof.\n\nRelated efforts include FluidLean,\nour team's Euler project, and\nOpenAI's Navier–Stokes and Euler formalizations.\nWe aim to contribute suitable general results to\nMathlib and to\ncoordinate with Tau Ceti's PDE roadmap.\n\nWe contacted Evans about following his book, and he was excited about the project.\n\nA few parts of the library we are especially excited about:\n\n* Constructing solutions. The\n  fundamental-solution construction\n  gives classical Poisson solutions for compactly supported  data in\n  dimensions . The\n  Dirichlet principle relates\n  classical solutions to finite-energy minimizers on bounded  graph domains.\n  These are complementary ways to use the analysis: one builds a formula,\n  the other identifies a solution by its energy.* Analysis beyond explicit formulas. The development also covers Sobolev\n  spaces and traces, compactness, weak formulations, heat and wave equations,\n  transport, and variational methods.* Calculations a proof can use. cas proves supported identities,\n  derivatives and integrals in ordinary Lean notation. cega turns intervals,\n  Taylor models, quadrature and root certificates into bounds on real functions.\n  We use FloatLib for checked interval\n  arithmetic and elementary-function enclosures. The\n  soundness theorems connect the\n  computed expressions to Mathlib's analytic definitions. We can differentiate\n  a proposed profile, check its residual, and use that result in an energy estimate.* Fluid constructions. Given a smooth harmonic velocity potential, we\n  construct an Euler solution with\n  recovered pressure and the prescribed initial trace. The fluid library also\n  treats weak formulations, vorticity, energy and exact flows. Two applications\n  built on it, blowup for the De Gregorio model on the real line and the\n  nonuniqueness of suitable Leray–Hopf solutions of the Navier–Stokes equations,\n  are formalized in the companion repository\n  BlowupZoo.\n\nThe CAS also responds to a long-standing community question. The\n2020 CAS-tactics wishlist\nasked for expression manipulation that sits naturally inside a Lean proof. In a\n2023 Lean Zulip discussion,\nKaiyu Yang proposed a transparent PDE solver, and the discussion asked how\nto turn that idea into a concrete plan. Our contribution is a set of symbolic procedures whose answers come\nwith proofs over the analytic objects a PDE argument already uses.\nHex develops verified computational algebra,\nincluding polynomial, matrix and algebraic-number algorithms with Mathlib\ncorrespondence layers where available.\nThe computation chapter explains our algorithms,\nprior work and supported operations.\n\nFor large finite computations, cega [fast] can check certificates by compiled\nevaluation. This additionally trusts Lean's compiler and native evaluation;\nCAS and bare cega retain kernel checking. The\ncomputation chapter explains both modes and\nhow to build the De Gregorio certificates with kernel evaluation instead.\n\nWe began thinking about this project in early 2024. That July, Robert\nasked on Lean Zulip\nabout PDE formalization. The conversation pointed us toward the multivariable\ncalculus and Sobolev theory already in Mathlib. Much of the work that followed\nwas learning that analysis, choosing definitions, and working through proofs.\nWe used AI coding agents to write and repair proofs and documentation. We chose the statements,\ndefinitions and sources, and checked the claims.\n\nPDEAtlas is written by Robert Joseph George and Anima Anandkumar at Caltech, with the PDE\ngroup of Thomas Y. Hou. Contributors include Peicong Song. We thank the Lean and Mathlib communities for their mathematics,\ntools and conversations, and Evans for his interest in the project.\nSource references identify the results we follow.\n\nTo contribute a PDE result, extend the computer algebra system, or improve an explanation, see\nthe contributing guide.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"","header":"PDEAtlas","id":"/#leanpde"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Linear-pushforward":{"contents":"Every continuous real-linear equivalence  induces a continuous\ncomplex-linear equivalencecharacterized byNo Jacobian factor belongs in this defining distributional pairing. A\nJacobian appears only after one chooses volume measures and rewrites the\npushforward of a regular distribution as another density.\n\nPushforward is contravariant on test functions and hence covariant on\ndistributions.\n\nLinear pushforward preserves identities and composition, and pushforward by\n is inverse to pushforward by :\n\nEach identity follows after evaluating on a Schwartz function. Identity and\ncomposition reduce to the corresponding laws for precomposition, while the\ninverse law uses .\n\nDifferentiating a pushforward requires transporting the direction back through\n: since  is differentiated along  when \nis differentiated along , the distributional statement carries .\n\nDirectional derivatives transform contravariantly under linear pushforward:\n\nEvaluate both sides on a Schwartz function and move the distributional\nderivative onto that test function. The ordinary chain rule gives\n, which is exactly the\ndisplayed identity after transposition.\n\nEquality rather than inclusion is special to invertible transport: the smooth\nproper pushforward of ordinary distributions gives only an inclusion.\n\nLinear pushforward carries distributional support to its geometric image:\n\nA pushforward vanishes on an open set  precisely when the original\ndistribution vanishes on . Since  is a homeomorphism, it maps\nthe family of open vanishing sets, and hence their union, to the corresponding\nfamily and union for . Taking complements gives the support identity.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Tempered Distributions","header":"3.4.2.3. Linear pushforward","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Linear-pushforward"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Conservation-and-Incompressibility--From-Conservation-Laws-to-Incompressible-Flow--From-Transport-to-Fluid-Motion":{"contents":"The simplest evolution in the hierarchy is a scalar carried by a prescribed\nvelocity. The transport equation\n\n\n\nsays that  is constant along particle paths. The velocity is known,\nso the equation is linear in the transported scalar. The\ncharacteristic chain\nrule is the scalar counterpart of the\nparticle-path chain rule.\n\nIn one space dimension, allowing the transported quantity to determine its\nown speed gives inviscid Burgers,\n. Characteristics can intersect, smooth\nsolutions can form shocks, and weak solutions then require an entropy\ncondition. Incompressible Euler retains the self-transport mechanism, but the\nvelocity is vector-valued and pressure enforces the divergence-free\nconstraint.\n\nA fluid is described in Eulerian variables by fields attached to positions in\nspace: density , velocity , and pressure . A\nparticle released at  follows a trajectory  satisfying\n\n\n\nIf a scalar or vector field  is observed along that particle path, the\nchain rule gives\n\n\n\nThis is the material derivative. For prescribed  it is linear in the\ntransported field . The fluid nonlinearity appears when , because\nthe unknown velocity then transports itself.\n\nA particle path is represented by the local identityThe pointwise predicate is useful when only a local calculation is needed.\nIts setwise form requires the same identity throughout a time interval.\n\nIf  is a particle path, the associated spacetime curve\n has velocity . Therefore every\ndifferentiable spacetime field  satisfiesFor scalar , expanding the Fréchet derivative gives the checked identity\n\nWrite the spacetime trajectory as the sum of its time coordinate and the\nzero-time embedding of . Their derivatives are respectively the time\nbasis vector and the embedded spatial velocity. The ordinary Fréchet chain\nrule gives the directional derivative, and expansion in the Euclidean\ncoordinate basis separates its time coordinate from the spatial sum.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Conservation and Incompressibility\u0009From Conservation Laws to Incompressible Flow","header":"8.2.1.1. From Transport to Fluid Motion","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Conservation-and-Incompressibility--From-Conservation-Laws-to-Incompressible-Flow--From-Transport-to-Fluid-Motion"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Analytic-Model-Methods":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods","header":"9.2.1.8. Analytic Model Methods","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Analytic-Model-Methods"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Analytic-Model-Methods--Power-series-and-analytic-model-methods--Prescribed-normal-data-on-an-analytic-hypersurface":{"contents":"For a scalar equation of order , the Cauchy data prescribe  normal\nderivatives. The normal is held fixed at each evaluation point:\n\n\n\nThus derivatives of the normal field itself do not enter the datum.\nExpanding this tensor in coordinates includes the usual mixed-partial\nmultiplicities, while the coefficients of the differential equation retain\ntheir original normalization.\n\nLet , let  with  open, and suppose\n is analytic near , , and\n. Let  be analytic near\n; their restrictions to  are the prescribed\ndata. Use the unit normal .\nConsider the scalar equationHere  records all derivatives below order . Let  be\nthe lower jet at  determined by the prescribed data. Assume the coefficient\ngerms are analytic at  and\n.\nThen some ball , , carries an analytic\nsolution of the equation with  on \nfor every . Two analytic solution germs with the same normal data\nagree near .\n\nConstruct an analytic level chart and interpolate the individual normal data\nto an analytic function. These constructions determine the lower jet, so\na compatible extension of the data need not be given. The nonzero principal\nsymbol lets the transformed equation solve for its highest transverse\nderivative. Reduction to analytic evolution gives a local solution, which\nis pulled back through the chart. Tangential differentiation of equal normal\ndata recovers the full lower jet, and analytic uniqueness gives germ equality.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Analytic Model Methods\u0009Power-series and analytic model methods","header":"9.2.1.8.1.2. Prescribed normal data on an analytic hypersurface","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Analytic-Model-Methods--Power-series-and-analytic-model-methods--Prescribed-normal-data-on-an-analytic-hypersurface"}});